#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:3
msgid ""
"The built-in sequence and wrapper surfaces: the heap array `[T]`, the "
"nullable `?T`, the growable `Vec<T>`, `Weak<T>`, and the keyed collections."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:7
msgid "`[T]` — the heap array"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:9
msgid ""
"`[T]` is the spelling of the fixed array: **runtime length, non-growable**. "
"The type is grammar, resolved directly — no `use` names it."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:13
msgid ""
"```rut\n"
"use ink::{ Logger };\n"
"\n"
"struct Point { x: i32; y: i32; }\n"
"\n"
"pub fn main() {\n"
"    let log = Logger.new(\"t\");\n"
"    let xs: [i32] = [1, 2, 3];   // the literal allocates the cell\n"
"    let ys: [?Point] = [nil; 4]; // the repeat: a VALUE and a count\n"
"    log.info(f\"{xs.len()} {ys.len()}\");\n"
"}\n"
"```"
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:30
msgid ""
"Construction is the **repeat expression** `[v; n]` — a value and a count; "
"there is no type-in-expression form. A scalar/`nil` fill is the memset-class "
"op; a ref fill retains the cell handle `n` times — every slot aliases the "
"one cell (the sharing law: the repeat never copies)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:35
msgid ""
"`[T]` is a cell handle — shared like every non-primitive: assignment "
"aliases, mutation is visible through every handle."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:37
msgid ""
"`a[i]`, `a[i] = x`, `.len()`, and `for (x of a)` are compiler-lowered to the "
"fused array ops — never a per-element call. Out-of-bounds traps."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:40
msgid ""
"Fixed-length windows: `v.slice(from, to)` — see [String slicing and "
"views](string-views.md)."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:43
msgid ""
"The old `Array<T>` name is removed: the array type is spelled `[T]`, "
"construction is the repeat `[v; n]`."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:48
msgid ""
"`?T` is a nil-able cell: a one-slot box whose payload is a `T` or the null "
"slot. `nil` is its null literal — and the empty type's one value: a "
"context-free `nil` has type `nil`, while nullable positions (`let p: ?T = "
"nil`, `p == nil`, `left: nil` in a literal) type it as `?T`. Dereferencing "
"`nil` is the `NilDeref` trap — never a silent read."
msgstr ""

#: src/reference/builtin-generic-types.md:54
msgid ""
"The spelling is **prefix-only and binds tightest** — `?` applies to the type "
"term that follows:"
msgstr ""

